# Dockerfile.dockerignore -- RAMEAU router build context
#
# Context root: services/router/ in this repository.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FILENAME IS LOAD-BEARING, AND IT WAS WRONG UNTIL 2026-11-02
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker reads an ignore-file from the BUILD CONTEXT ROOT, or as
# `<dockerfile-name>.dockerignore` beside the Dockerfile -- and nowhere else. This file lives
# in deploy/images/router/ while the context is services/router/, so for as long as it was
# named plain `.dockerignore` it was read by NOTHING, and every pattern below was inert:
# `COPY *.py ./` was bounded by nothing, and the "never, under any circumstances" block at
# the end of this file -- .env, *.pem, *.key, .netrc, .git-credentials -- was not in force at
# all.
#
# Measured rather than reasoned about. A `test_probe.py`, which the list below excludes, was
# planted in a stub context: it arrived in /app of the built image. Named to match the
# Dockerfile, as it now is, the same probe is excluded.
#
# So do not rename this file back, and do not move it without moving the Dockerfile with it.
# `git mv`-ing either one alone disarms this entire file, silently -- and the warning further
# down, that a green build is not evidence this list is right, applies to its NAME first.
#
# Deny-by-default, then re-admit. The point is not image size, it is that a narrow context is
# the only thing that makes the COPY groups in the Dockerfile mean anything: `COPY *.py ./`
# cannot widen what this file has already excluded.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO SYNTAX RULES THIS FILE OBEYS, BOTH LEARNED THE HARD WAY
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. NO TRAILING COMMENTS ON A PATTERN LINE. A '#' starts a comment only at the START of a
#    line. Writing
#        !fleet_config.json              # read by config.py
#    is the single pattern "fleet_config.json<spaces>#<spaces>read by config.py", which
#    matches nothing, so the re-admission silently does not happen.
# 2. A NEGATION CANNOT RE-ADMIT A FILE WHOSE PARENT DIRECTORY IS EXCLUDED. `**` followed by
#    `!dir/file` does not work; the directory has to be re-admitted first.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A GREEN BUILD IS NOT EVIDENCE THIS LIST IS RIGHT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A module missing from this list does not fail the build. It fails at import or at first
# use, and /status can still answer 200. After every refresh of the router source, recompute
# the import closure of main:app with an AST walk and compare it against what this admits.
#
# And one module is NOT in that closure and must still be admitted: bifrost_delegate.py is
# read off disk with `ast` and imported by nothing. Omit it and the tool loop refuses every
# delegated leaf, fail-closed, while health still reads green. It is admitted explicitly
# below, with this comment, because it is the one entry a closure-based regeneration of this
# file would drop.
#
# SUSPENDED 2026-11-02: the ruled router source does not contain bifrost_delegate.py, so
# there is nothing for this admission to admit. The `!*.py` line already covers it if it
# ever arrives, so nothing is removed and no pattern changes -- but do not treat this
# paragraph as evidence the file exists. See RELEASE-SCOPE.md.

# ---- deny everything, then re-admit ----
**

# ---- dependency manifest ----
!requirements.txt

# ---- application source ----
# Bounded to the top level on purpose: the router's modules are flat, and `**/*.py` would
# re-admit test files, scratch scripts and vendored copies.
!*.py

# ---- packages ----
!backends/
!backends/**

# ---- the skill registry, baked at build time ----
!skills/
!skills/**
# The endpoint registry, for layer 3b. This context is deny-all (`**` above) with explicit
# allowances, so without these two lines `COPY shared/` has nothing to copy.
#
# MEASURED 2026-11-13, because the guess was wrong and the wrong guess was the comfortable
# one. Removing these two lines and rebuilding does NOT silently produce an image with an
# empty /app/shared -- which is what this comment claimed until it was tested, and which
# would have meant a router failing at import on `from shared.resolver import resolve`.
# buildkit fails the build instead:
#
#     COPY shared/ /app/shared/
#     ERROR: failed to compute cache key: ... "/shared": not found
#
# So this allowance is load-bearing and its absence is loud. Good -- but the pleasant
# behaviour is the measured one, not the assumed one, and it is recorded here so the next
# person does not have to re-derive it.
!shared/
!shared/**

# ---- NOT admitted, and deliberately so ----
# The four runtime JSON files are absent from this context by design. The release ships them
# as *.example.json and the stack bind-mounts the operator's copies; see the Dockerfile and
# deploy/compose.yaml. Do not re-admit them: baking fleet detail into a published image is
# the thing the release exists to avoid.
#
# NAMED AS PATTERNS, not left to `**`, and recursive -- added 2026-11-03. This section was
# prose only, which meant the four files were kept out at the CONTEXT ROOT and nowhere else:
# `backends/**` and `skills/**` above re-admit wholesale, and `**` has already been
# overridden by the time a file inside them is considered. Measured on this file AS IT
# STOOD BEFORE 04a4eb1 -- it is byte-identical from 1d69a59 through 5bb4983, so any
# of those five commits shows the same bytes: `backends/fleet_config.json`,
# `backends/capability_profiles.json`, `backends/bifrost_shell_policy.json` and
# `skills/capability_registry.json` all arrived in the built image while the root
# `fleet_config.json` was correctly excluded. A reader testing the current file will and
# should see the opposite.
#
# The four are named ONE BY ONE rather than as a blanket `**/*.json`, for two reasons and
# only the second applies to this list. First, on the PORTAL's list a blanket would sit
# below `!package.json` and `!package-lock.json` and deny the portal its own manifest --
# this list re-admits no JSON at all, so that hazard is the other file's, and an earlier
# draft of this comment wrongly generalised it to both by citing the `!requirements.txt`
# re-admission, which is not JSON. Second, and the reason here: naming them is the only
# form that says WHICH files this is about, and the four are the subject of note 1 in the
# Dockerfile and of all four deploy/config/*.example.json.
**/fleet_config.json
**/capability_registry.json
**/capability_profiles.json
**/bifrost_shell_policy.json

# ---- never, under any circumstances ----
# THIS BLOCK'S GUARANTEE RESTS ON THREE PROPERTIES, AND ALL THREE ARE NOW GATED.
# Corrected 2026-11-03 on the merge that made it true; both sides of that merge still
# said "NOT gated" about two of them, each side blind to the step the other had added.
#   REACH     -- the patterns are recursive, so they apply inside the subtrees this file
#                re-admits wholesale.                   Fixed 1d69a59.
#                GATED by "prove both ignore files' patterns are in force" in the `images`
#                job: it builds a PLANTED context (`FROM scratch`, `COPY . /ctx/`,
#                `--output type=local`) and asserts the transferred file list exactly.
#                That step asserts the PATTERNS; A1 of the step-4 preflight asserts the
#                LOCATION. NEITHER IS SUFFICIENT ALONE, and the probes this comment used
#                to apologise for -- throwaway stub contexts, built outside git and
#                discarded -- have been replaced by a step that runs on every push.
#   FINALITY  -- nothing re-admits below this block, because a .dockerignore is
#                last-match-wins.                       Fixed and GATED in 04a4eb1.
#   SHAPE COVERAGE -- the shapes listed below are the ones worth denying.
#                GATED by "the never-block must cover the declared shape set" in the
#                `build` job (tools/check_never_block_shapes.py), which is what finally
#                replaced the prose this heading used to rest on.
#
# ONE RESIDUAL RISK SURVIVES ALL THREE, and it is not closed by any of them: `docker build`
# reads the WORKING TREE rather than the index, so a credential that is merely gitignored is
# invisible to every git-based gate here and still enters the context. It is recorded in
# RELEASE-SCOPE.md as the top residual risk rather than patched in passing, because closing
# it is a decision about what this list is FOR.
#
# Every pattern here is RECURSIVE (`**/`), and NONE of them was until 2026-11-03. That is
# the whole point of the block rather than a style choice: the stated purpose is that a
# re-admission above cannot sweep one of these in as a side effect -- and `!backends/**`
# and `!skills/**` above are exactly such re-admissions, wholesale. A single `*` does not
# cross a '/', so `.env` and `*.pem` were matched against ONE PATH SEGMENT: they denied a
# top-level `.env` and said nothing whatever about `backends/.env`. The side effect this
# block exists to prevent was the state this block was in.
#
# MEASURED, not reasoned about. In a planted context built with `FROM scratch`,
# `COPY . /ctx/` and `--output type=local`: `backends/.env`, `backends/leak.pem`,
# `skills/leak.key` and `backends/sub.log` were all transferred with the single-segment
# patterns and none of them is with these. `COPY backends/ /app/backends/` and
# `COPY skills/ /app/skills/` copy whole directories, so those four would have reached the
# published image. `**/__pycache__/` and `**/*.pyc` were already written this way, which is
# exactly why they were the only two entries in this block that held at depth.
#
# `**/P` also matches a top-level `P` -- measured on docker 29.9.2 -- so these REPLACE the
# bare forms rather than joining them. Do not "restore" the bare forms; they are subsumed.
# The probe that measures all of this lives in the `images` job of .github/workflows/ci.yml
# and runs on every push, with or without a source tree.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/id_rsa
**/id_ed25519
**/.netrc
**/.git-credentials
**/.git/
**/.github/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.log
**/*.bak
**/*.bak-*
**/*.sqlite
**/*.db
**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/node_modules/
**/*.env
**/secrets/
**/.secrets/
**/*.secret
**/*.secrets
**/credentials.json
**/*credential*
**/*-credentials*
**/auth.json
**/.sops.yaml
**/.sops/
**/*.kubeconfig
**/kubeconfig
**/kubeconfig.*
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/id_rsa.*
**/id_ed25519.*
**/*.ppk
**/known_hosts
**/*.token
**/_netrc
**/.npmrc
**/.pypirc

# ---- top-level only, and DELIBERATELY not generalised ----
# These three are the asymmetry, and it is the point rather than an oversight. A pattern that
# denies too much drops a module that something imports, and that fails inside the container
# after a green build -- which is the failure class B4 of the step-4 preflight exists to
# prevent, and it is worse than the thing generalising these would buy.
#
# `tests/` here is the suite at the context root. A `backends/tests/` is re-admitted by
# `!backends/**`, named by `COPY backends/` and does arrive in the image: that is weight, not
# a credential. A `**/test_*.py` that dropped an imported `test_utils.py` would be the worse
# trade. B4's comment in ci.yml says these are "denied deliberately"; that is true at the
# context root and NOT true one directory down, and the preflight's exclusions are still
# correct for a different reason -- such files arrive rather than being dropped twice.
tests/
test_*.py
*_test.py
